
		                 GnuCOBOL
		https://www.gnu.org/software/gnucobol/
		https://sourceforge.net/projects/gnucobol
		https://savannah.gnu.org/projects/gnucobol

See README for general instructions and DEPENDENCIES for general requirements.

============
Development
============

If you wish to hack the GnuCOBOL source code, proceed as follows. The
following extra packages need to be installed with versions equal or greater.

If you build from VCS the tools in the first 4 lists are always needed.

For running "autogen.sh" (necessary after VCS checkout) / reconfigure:
  o autoconf 2.70
  o automake 1.16
  o libtool 2.2.6 (2.5.3 highly+ suggested, can be easily installed locally)
  o m4 1.4.12

If you modify top-level configure.ac or Makefile.am in any directory and rerun
"make", the build system will regenerate the necessary files.

If you want to update to a different automake/libtool version or get errors
about wrong version numbers in m4 run "autogen.sh install" instead.

For compiling (when changing pparser/scanner sources):
  o Bison 2.3     (will be changed to 3.6 with GnuCOBOL 4)
  o Flex 2.5.35

For generating the testsuite (when changing any .at files):
  o autoconf 2.64

For generating the translation files (when changing any msgid):
  o gettext 0.17
    ** NOTE **
    If you don't need an up-to-date translation you can do
    "touch po/*.pot po/*.po po/*.gmo" instead.

For generating the documentation (necessary for preparing a distribution):
  o help2man 0.17
    ** NOTE **
    If you don't need an up-to-date manpage you can do
    "touch cobc/cobc.1 bin/cobcrun.1" instead.
  o texinfo 6.1 (with texinfo-tex)
  o texlive (latest, texlive-collection-latex suffices)
    ** NOTE **
    If you don't need an up-to-date info source / manual you can do
    "touch doc/gnucobol.info doc/gnucobol.pdf" instead.


For more information about some of the internals and a coding guideline see
https://sourceforge.net/p/gnucobol/wiki/Style%20guide/ and
https://sourceforge.net/p/gnucobol/wiki/For%20Maintainers/


For preparing a distribution do the following steps:
  ./configure           # check that the current environment is ok and
                        # generate Makefiles for distclean
  make distclean        # only needed if it isn't a direct VCS checkout
                        # --> start with a clean directory
  ./configure && make   # build, needed for re-generating bison/flex sources
                        # and for generating the texi-includes later
  po/update_linguas.sh  # get latest translations
  make distcheck        # generate a distribution tarball and check
                        # that it will work in a VPATH environment
                        # including make targets uninstall, distclean and dist

Most often there is a minimal supported version (for example GnuCOBOL 3.x
should work with GnuCOBOL versions back to 2.2); the easiest way to test
that is to first have the old version installed installed or build locally,
then do a manual run of hand-picked "special" tests from the version to test,
for example:

  make check TESTSUITEFLAGS="--debug -k exception-location -k trace"

which keeps the test sources because of "--debug".

Then do for each test you're interested in the following:
 pushd tests/testsuite.dir/NNNN  # go into the test directory
 ./run                           # re-execute the test verbose with
                                 # the version under test
 cobc --debug XXXXX              # execute all of the compile commands
                                 # with the old active environment
OR                               # if you want to compare with an
                                 # uninstalled version:
 /some/path/pre-inst-env cobc --debug XXXXX
 ../../../pre-inst-env prog      # execute the old-compiled programs
                                 # with the version under test
 popd                            # back to root...
 
And when running compare the output to the one seen when executing ./run.

For an unspecific test that does not check any of the "newer" features or
extensions the NIST suite can be compiled with that "old active" environment
while being executed with the version under test.

 cd tests/cobol85
 make test-local-compat -j6      # compile with the old active environment
                                 # but run with the new version under test
OR                               # if you want to compare with an
                                 # uninstalled version:
 make test-local-compat COBC="/some/path/pre-inst-env cobc"

There should be no difference shown as long as the "old version" passed
the same tests as the new version - given the example above: there was
no support for REPORT WRITER in 2.2 so there would be an expected failure
of the RW tests.


===================
Coding Style Guide
===================

# General

* Tabs are used for indentation
* Tab stops are at every 8 columns from column 1
* All names are in snake_case.
* **D**on't **R**epeat **Y**ourself
* Avoid writing functions longer than 2 screens (~70 lines)
* Try to keep lines less than 80 characters long
* Names should be as informative as possible; avoid variable names such
  as `i`, `j`, `f`, `p`, etc. in all but the most trivial situations
* Use `svn diff` to produce patches, rather than individually `diff`-ing
  files, as they can be quickly applied using `svn patch`.

# Common abbreviations
Avoid using abbreviations, where possible. Especially avoid using multiple
abbreviations in one name.

	Abbreviation | Meaning
	------------ | ----------
	adv          | advancing
	arg          | argument
	attr         | attribute
	backg        | background
	bgc          | background-color
	ch           | char(acter)
	col          | column
	conf         | configuration
	cur(r)       | current
	decl         | declarative
	dir          | directory
	div          | division
	dst          | destination
	dup(ed)      | duplicate(d)
	env          | environment
	err          | error
	ex           | exception
	ext          | external
	f            | field/file
	fgc          | foreground-color
	fmt          | format
	foreg        | foreground
	gen          | generate
	hex          | hexadecimal
	idx          | index
	init         | initialize
	intr         | intrinsic
	len          | length
	lex          | lexer
	lit          | literal
	lst          | list
	msg          | message
	num          | number (of)
	odo          | `OCCURS DEPENDING ON`
	optim        | optimize
	opt          | optimize
	orig         | original
	org          | original
	p            | pointer
	_p           | predicate
	para         | paragraph
	param        | parameter
	prm          | parameter
	proc         | procedure
	prog         | program
	ptr          | pointer
	refmod       | reference modification
	rep          | replacing/replacement
	src          | source
	stmt         | statement
	str          | string
	tbl          | table
	temp         | temporary
	val          | value
	var(s)       | variable(s)
	warn         | warning

# C
* Always use `/* C89 block */` comments
* Comments are *preferred*  to be  be on their own line
* Brace style is strict "One True Brace Style"
```c
/* Correct */
if (x == 1) {
	do_something ();
} else {
	do_something_else ();
}

/* Incorrect */
if (x == 1) do_something ();
if (x == 1)
	do_something ();
if (x == 1) { do_something (); }
```
 * Do not use assignments in `if` conditions
 * In function declarations, the return type and the name go on separate lines
 * Variables are declared at the beginning of the block using them
 * Variable names in declaration should be aligned with a tab
 * If possible, merge initialization into declaration
 * One variable is declared per line when it includes the initialization, 
   otherwise mulitple variables of the same type may be declared in one line
 * reduce scope of vars where possible
```c

void
incorrect (void)
{
	const char *str;
  int x;

	if (a == b) {
		x = a;
		do_something (&x);
		struct cb_field *x_field = make_field (x);
		str = to_string (x_field);
	}
	/* further use of str ... */
}

void
correct (void)
{
	const char	*str = NULL;

	if (a == b) {
		int 			x = a;
		struct cb_field	*x_field;

		do_something (&x);
		x_field = make_field (x);
		str = to_string (x_field);
	}
	
	/* further use of str ... */
}
```

 * Macro names are in ANGRY_SNAKE_CASE.
 * *`#undef` minor, local macros after last use*
 * Global names in `cobc` are prefixed with:
	* `cobc_`, if they are defined in `cobc.c`;
	* `plex_`, if they are defined in `pplex.l`;
	* `pp`, if they are defined in `ppparse.y`;
	* `ylex_`, if they are defined in `scanner.l`;
	* `yy`, if they are defined in `parser.y`;
	* `cb_`, if they are an enum or constant from anywhere else;
	* optionally `cb_`, for anything else.
 * Global names in `libcob` are prefixed with `cob_`
 * Spaces around arithmetic operators
 * One space before (the first in a series of) an opening parenthesis

```c
/* Correct */
if ((arr[x + 1] + 2) / 10 > 5) {
	/* ... */
}

/* Incorrect */
if( (arr[x+1]+2)/10 > 5 ) {
	/* ... */
}
```
 
 * *No space after casts*
 * Fix all warnings before committing a patch.
 * Use enums instead of lists of macros.

## Memory Management
 * Use project allocators: `cob_malloc`, `cob_free`, `cob_strdup`
 * Nullify pointers after freeing only for non-local variables or variables\n   used later
```c
/* Correct - non-local variable */
if (cobsetptr->cob_preload_str) {
	cob_free ((void *)cobsetptr->cob_preload_str);
	cobsetptr->cob_preload_str = NULL;
}

/* Correct - local variable used later */
void
some_function (void)
{
	char	*buffer;
	
	buffer = cob_malloc (size);
	/* ... use buffer ... */
	cob_free (buffer);
	buffer = NULL;  /* Will be used again later */
	/* ... more code that might use buffer ... */
}

/* Unnecessary - local variable at end of scope */
void
other_function (void)
{
	char	*temp;
	
	temp = cob_malloc (size);
	/* ... use temp ... */
	cob_free (temp);
	/* No need to nullify - function ends */
}
```

## Field Access Patterns
 * Use consistent field data access macros
```c
/* Correct */
const unsigned char	*data = COB_FIELD_DATA (field);
const size_t		size = COB_FIELD_SIZE (field);

/* Avoid direct access */
const unsigned char	*data = field->data;  /* Don't do this */
```

## Static Function Organization
 * Static functions before public functions
 * Group related static functions together
 * Use forward declarations only when needed for mutual recursion
```c
/* Static helper functions first */
static int
helper_function (const cob_field *field)
{
	/* ... */
}

static void
another_helper (void)
{
	/* ... */
}

/* Public functions after */
int
cob_public_function (cob_field *field)
{
	return helper_function (field);
}
```

## Const Correctness
 * Use `const` for read-only parameters
 * Use `const` for string literals and lookup tables
 * Prefer `const char *` over `char *` when data won't be modified
```c
/* Function parameters */
int
cob_compare_fields (const cob_field *f1, const cob_field *f2)

/* Lookup tables */
static const struct optim_table	bin_compare_funcs[] = {
	{ "cob_cmp_u8",		COB_CMP_U8 },
	{ "cob_cmp_u16",	COB_CMP_U16 },
	/* ... */
};

/* String handling */
const char	*get_field_name (const cob_field *field);  /* Returns read-only */
char		*format_field (cob_field *field);         /* Returns modifiable */
```

## Documentation Standards
 * Document the purpose ("why"), not the implementation ("what")
 * Use this format for complex functions:
```c
/*
 * Brief function description
 * 
 * Parameters:
 *   field - description of parameter
 *   param - description of parameter
 * 
 * Returns:
 *   description of return value, error conditions
 * 
 * Note: any important implementation details or side effects
 */
int
cob_complex_function (const cob_field *field, const int param)
{
	/* ... */
}
```

## Backwards Compatibility
 * **Never change existing function signatures** in public APIs
 * **Never remove public functions** - mark as deprecated instead
 * **Never change behavior** of existing functions in ways that break\n   existing programs
 * Add new functionality through:
   * New functions with different names
   * New optional parameters (with sensible defaults)
   * New configuration options (defaulting to old behavior)
 * Use `#ifdef` guards for new features that might not be available on\n   all systems
 * Maintain ABI compatibility within major versions
```c
/* Correct - adding new function */
int cob_new_feature (cob_field *field);

/* Incorrect - changing existing function */
int cob_existing_function (cob_field *field, int new_param);\n                                                    /* DON'T DO THIS */

/* Correct - extending with optional behavior */
int cob_existing_function_ex (cob_field *field, int options);

/* Correct - deprecation pattern */
#ifndef COB_WITHOUT_DEPRECATED
int cob_old_function (cob_field *field) COB_DEPRECATED;
#endif
```

# Bison

* Optional syntax elements are prefixed with `_`.
* Token are indented with *two spaces*. (Code is still indented with a tab.)

```c
hello_statement:
  HELLO _world
  {
	cb_emit_hello ($2);
  }
;

_world:
  /* empty */
  {
	$$ = NULL;
  }
| WORLD
  {
	$$ = $1;
  }
;
```

# COBOL
(For testsuite entries.)

* Code is in fixed-format.
* Environment division and procedure division code is indented by four spaces.
* Reserved words and intrinsic function names are in uppercase.
* *User-defined words can be in any case*
* The words `DIVISION` and `SECTION` should be aligned
* A data division which contains more than a few entries should align\n  their clauses
* Tests should check that valid code is accepted and that invalid code\n  is rejected.
* Short run tests may display the output of what they are testing and use\n  `AT_CHECK` to check that the output is correct
* Longer tests may test the output inside the program and, if it is invalid,\n  indicate which check failed and how.
```cobol
       *> This example is from the TEST-NUMVAL-C test.
       IDENTIFICATION   DIVISION.
       PROGRAM-ID.      prog.
       DATA             DIVISION.
       WORKING-STORAGE  SECTION.
       PROCEDURE        DIVISION.
           IF FUNCTION TEST-NUMVAL-C ("+ 1")     NOT = 0
              DISPLAY "Test 1  fail"
              END-DISPLAY
           END-IF.
           IF FUNCTION TEST-NUMVAL-C (" + 1")    NOT = 0
              DISPLAY "Test 2  fail"
              END-DISPLAY
           END-IF.
           IF FUNCTION TEST-NUMVAL-C ("- 1")     NOT = 0
              DISPLAY "Test 3  fail"
              END-DISPLAY
           END-IF.
           *> ...
```
* In any case, a developer must be able to quickly find what is failing\n  from the `testsuite.log`.

# ChangeLog entries

* Please leave the empty line at the top of change logs as this helps\n  in merging.
* Each major directory maintains its own ChangeLog with this format:

~~~~
YYYY-MM-DD  John Smith <johnsmith@whatever.org>

	* file1.c (the_one_func_that_was_changed): moved check from the beginning
	  to the end.
	* file2.c, file3.h: refactored lots of things in lots of places.

~~~~

* Each bullet is indented with one tab.
* **Special case**: `tests/ChangeLog` only documents structural changes (new test files, framework changes), not individual test case additions.

# Multi-line Conditions

Multi-line conditions use a special indentation pattern to align operators:

```c
/* Correct - tab + spaces to align operators */
if ((gc_conf[i].data_type & ENV_STR)
||  (gc_conf[i].data_type & ENV_FILE)
||  (gc_conf[i].data_type & ENV_PATH)) {
	/* body */
}

/* Another example */
if (cobsetptr->cob_dump_file == cobsetptr->cob_trace_file
 || cobsetptr->cob_dump_file == stderr) {
	cobsetptr->cob_dump_file = NULL;
}
```

The pattern is:
1. First line: normal tab indentation + condition
2. Continuation lines: tab indentation + spaces to align operators (`||`, `&&`) at same column
3. Opening brace: same indentation as `if`

# Function Structure

Functions should follow this structure:

```c
static int
cob_example_function (const cob_field *field, const int param)
{
	/* Variable declarations at start of block */
	int		result;
	size_t		size;
	const char	*data;
	
	/* Input validation */
	if (!field) {
		return -1;
	}
	
	/* Main logic with conditional compilation when needed */
#ifdef HAVE_SOME_FEATURE
	result = optimized_operation (field, param);
#else
	result = fallback_operation (field, param);
#endif
	
	return result;
}
```

Key points:
* Return type and function name on separate lines
* Variables declared at start of block, aligned with tabs
* Logical grouping with comments
* Consistent error handling patterns
